## Solution

**Step 1: Determine $f(1)$.** Since $f$ is strictly increasing, $f(1) \geq 1$. If $f(1)=1$ then $f(f(1))=f(1)=1\neq 3$, contradiction. So $f(1)\geq 2$. Trying $f(1)=2$ gives $f(2)=f(f(1))=3$, which is consistent with strict increase ($2<3$). (One can show $f(1)\geq 3$ is impossible: $f(1)=a\geq 3$ forces $f(2),\dots,f(a-1)$ to lie in $(a,3)$, an empty interval when $a\geq 3$.) Hence $f(1)=2,\ f(2)=3$.

**Step 2: Discover the block structure.** From $f(2)=3$: $f(f(2))=f(3)=6$. Then $f(6)=9,\ f(9)=18,\ f(18)=27,\dots$, giving
$$f(3^k)=2\cdot 3^k,\qquad f(2\cdot 3^k)=3^{k+1}.$$
Filling gaps (strict increase forces the intermediate values):
- $f(3)=6,\ f(6)=9$ $\Rightarrow$ $f(4)=7,\ f(5)=8$.
- $f(7)=f(f(4))=12,\ f(8)=f(f(5))=15$.

This reveals a partition of $\mathbb{N}$ into blocks $B_k=[3^k,3^{k+1})$, each split as
$$L_k=[3^k,\,2\cdot 3^k),\qquad R_k=[2\cdot 3^k,\,3^{k+1}).$$

**Step 3: Closed form.** Define
$$\boxed{\;f(n)=n+3^k \text{ for } n\in L_k,\qquad f(n)=3n-3^{k+1} \text{ for } n\in R_k.\;}$$

**Step 4: Verify $f(f(n))=3n$.**

- **$n\in L_k$:** $f(n)=n+3^k\in R_k$ (since $n+3^k\in[2\cdot 3^k,3^{k+1})$). Then
$$f(f(n))=3(n+3^k)-3^{k+1}=3n+3^{k+1}-3^{k+1}=3n.\ \checkmark$$

- **$n\in R_k$:** $f(n)=3n-3^{k+1}$. Since $n\in[2\cdot 3^k,3^{k+1})$, we get $f(n)\in[3^{k+1},\,2\cdot 3^{k+1})=L_{k+1}$. Then
$$f(f(n))=(3n-3^{k+1})+3^{k+1}=3n.\ \checkmark$$

**Step 5: Verify strict increase.** Within each piece $f$ is linear with positive slope. At the internal boundary $n=2\cdot 3^k$: left value $f(2\cdot 3^k-1)=3^{k+1}-1$, right value $f(2\cdot 3^k)=3^{k+1}$; jump $+1$. At the block boundary $n=3^{k+1}$: left value $f(3^{k+1}-1)=2\cdot 3^{k+1}-3$, right value $f(3^{k+1})=2\cdot 3^{k+1}$; jump $+3$. All positive. $\checkmark$

**Step 6: Compute $f(2023)$.** We have $3^6=729$, $3^7=2187$, $2\cdot 3^6=1458$. Since
$$1458 \leq 2023 < 2187,$$
we have $2023\in R_6$. Therefore
$$f(2023)=3\cdot 2023-3^7=6069-2187=\boxed{3882}.$$

